# Paths that require review regardless of what CI says.
#
# The instrument guard (scripts/instrument_guard.py, enforced by .github/workflows/) already
# blocks a submission that touches any of this, and the evaluator already runs its own copy of
# the instrument rather than the submission's -- so none of it can raise a submission's own
# score. This file is the second lock, and it exists because the first one is a check that a
# pull request could otherwise disable in the same commit that needs disabling.
#
# Two independent mechanisms, failing in different ways: a check can be edited, an ownership
# rule is enforced by the forge. Neither is sufficient alone.

# --- what is measured -------------------------------------------------------
# Cells, shapes, tolerances, objectives, the sampling plan, the noise floors, the model pins.
# Overlaid from the base commit before scoring, so an edit cannot help its author -- it changes
# what everyone AFTER them is measured against.
/eval/                      @coderbench
/configs/                   @coderbench
/schemas/                   @coderbench
/tools/burnish              @coderbench

# --- how the evaluation is governed -----------------------------------------
# The guard, the checks that enforce it, the declared scoring model, and this file.
/.github/                   @coderbench
/.gittensor/                @coderbench
/scripts/                   @coderbench

# --- contributor surface, explicitly ----------------------------------------
# Build scripts are NOT owned: a submission that adds a source file may need them, and they
# cannot manufacture a speedup (base and candidate are two registered implementations of one
# binary, so a compiler flag moves both arms equally). They ARE code execution on the eval box,
# which is inherent to building from source -- isolate the box, do not rely on this file.
/scripts/build.sh
/scripts/build_cuda.sh
